Genode on the Google Pixel 7 (panther, Tensor G2 / gs201)

This repository contains

* the board support for the base-hw kernel,
* the run-tool plugin that packages a Genode image as an Android boot image,
  and
* the drivers for the phone, native Genode drivers for the GS201 SoC and
  ports of the vendor's Linux drivers.

Documentation
-------------

* doc/why_pixel.md           Why this phone, current state
* doc/device_preparation.md  Unlocking the boot loader, serial console


Note on authorship
------------------

This port is heavily LLM-assisted: large parts of the code were written with
the help of an AI coding assistant (Claude) and reviewed and tested by the
author. Please take this into account when reusing the code or considering
it for projects that do not accept LLM-assisted contributions.


Usage
-----

The repository is meant to be placed at 'repos/pixel' within the Genode
source tree. The following steps are executed in the root directory of the
Genode source tree. They require the Genode tool chain.

Create a build directory for the 64-bit ARM architecture:

! tool/create_builddir arm_v8a

Add the repository to the build configuration 'build/arm_v8a/etc/build.conf'.
The 'REPOSITORIES' line must appear before the line that adds 'base-$(KERNEL)'.
The second line selects the image plugin for the board:

! REPOSITORIES += $(GENODE_DIR)/repos/pixel
! BOARD_RUN_OPT(google_pixel7) := --include image/pixel_boot_img

The drivers additionally need the repositories 'libports' and 'dde_linux',
which are enabled by uncommenting their 'REPOSITORIES' lines in the same
file.

The image plugin and the drivers are built from third-party code provided by
ports. The kernel is a large download. The build names any missing port
together with the 'prepare_port' command that downloads it.

The device tree of a driver is compiled by the 'dtc' tool, which must be
installed on the host.

Build a scenario, e.g., 'run/log'. The result is the Android boot image
'var/run/<scenario>.img' within the build directory:

! cd build/arm_v8a
! make run/log KERNEL=hw BOARD=google_pixel7

To start the image, the boot loader of the phone must be unlocked. Bring the
phone into the fastboot mode (hold volume-down while powering on, or execute
'adb reboot bootloader' from a running Android), connect it via USB, and boot
the image without flashing it:

! fastboot boot var/run/log.img

The output of Genode appears at the debug UART of the phone (115200 baud).

Each driver has a README in its directory under 'src/driver', which names
the scenario that tests it.

The Linux-based drivers combine Genode's device-driver environment for
Linux with files of this repository that fit the 6.1 kernel of the phone,
see 'src/lx_emul_6_1/README'. After an update of the Genode source tree, the
following tool tells whether the files taken from Genode have changed:

! repos/pixel/tool/check_dde_drift build/arm_v8a

For repeated tests without touching the phone, the kernel can be built with
a serial reset monitor. With the build argument 'REBOOT=serial', the line
"RST" sent to the debug UART reboots the phone into its boot loader, where
the next image can be started via fastboot:

! make run/log KERNEL=hw BOARD=google_pixel7 REBOOT=serial


